    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\nImplement URL search synchronization as a small, typed responsibility with explicit inputs, outputs, state ownership, and edge-case handling.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nFirst define the state/data model, then implement one small pure operation, then connect it to the UI or side effect. Keep the source of truth in one place and derive everything else from it.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nWrite the loops, conditionals, immutable updates, or small helper logic explicitly so the algorithm is easy to explain in an interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Item {\n  name: string;\n  role: string;\n}\n\nfunction searchItems(\n  items: readonly Item[],\n  term: string,\n): Item[] {\n  const query = term.trim().toLowerCase();\n  const result: Item[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += 1) {\n    const item = items[i];\n\n    if (\n      item.name.toLowerCase().includes(query) ||\n      item.role.toLowerCase().includes(query)\n    ) {\n      result.push(item);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n\nconst result = searchItems(\n  [\n    { name: \"Alice\", role: \"Developer\" },\n    { name: \"Bob\", role: \"Designer\" },\n  ],\n  \"dev\",\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse the native array, collection, date, or browser helper when it makes the implementation clearer without hiding an important algorithmic idea.\n\n```ts\nconst items = [\n  { name: \"Alice\", role: \"Developer\" },\n  { name: \"Bob\", role: \"Designer\" },\n];\n\nconst query = \"dev\";\n\nconst result = items.filter((item) =>\n  `${item.name} ${item.role}`\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .includes(query.toLowerCase()),\n);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nKeep the URL search synchronization logic isolated from rendering and external side effects so it can be unit-tested independently.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity:\nThe exact complexity depends on the collection size or interaction frequency. The manual examples deliberately expose the loop or recursion so the time/space cost can be explained; the built-in form expresses the same intent more concisely.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty data, duplicate IDs, rapid interaction, slow/failed network, keyboard-only input, small screens, and large datasets when relevant to this problem.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the state is owned here, what can be reused, what should remain controlled by the parent, and what you would optimize only after measuring.",
